use std
into mesh_surf.amni
dialect js

This file is a library built for the web as a wasm module; its `main()` does nothing. JavaScript drives it through its exported functions (every name starting with ms_ is exported). It is a port of the triangle-surface core of cad/mesh.js: welding a triangle soup into a surface with normals, areas, centroids, edge adjacency and angle-weighted vertex normals, the bounding volume hierarchy over boxes, and the queries on it (closest point with filters, ray cast with filters, signed distance, wall thickness, octree cell classification and closest feature segment). Results are compared bit for bit with the JavaScript, so every floating-point formula keeps the JavaScript evaluation order exactly: each sum adds its terms one at a time in the order written, products and quotients group left to right as written, a negation is a real negation (never 0.0 minus a value), and nothing is fused, reordered or vectorized. Reals are f64.

```amni
extern fn ms_view_f64(id i32, xs real[:]) = "ms_view_f64";
extern fn ms_view_i32(id i32, xs i32[:]) = "ms_view_i32";
extern fn ms_view_u8(id i32, xs u8[:]) = "ms_view_u8";
extern fn ms_acos(x real) real = "ms_acos";
extern fn ms_log2(x real) real = "ms_log2";
```

Module state. Ints, all starting at 0: `NT` (triangles in the current triangle set), `NG` (segments in the current segment set), `NN` (node counter of the hierarchy being built). Growable arrays: f64 `P`, `V`, `N`, `AR`, `C`, `VN`, `NL`, `NH`, `GA`, `GNL`, `GNH`, `BL`, `BH`, `CE`, `KX`, `KY`, `KZ`, `HS`, `TH`, `TC`, `R`; i32 `I`, `T`, `TT`, `ORD`, `BS`, `BC`, `GL`, `GO`, `GS`, `GC`, `TJ`, `ST`, `W`, `VM`, `HT`, `EV`; int `EK`; u8 `MK`. Triangle t of the current triangle set has the vertex indices `T`[3t], `T`[3t+1], `T`[3t+2], and vertex v sits at `V`[3v], `V`[3v+1], `V`[3v+2]; that set's hierarchy is `ORD`, `NL`, `NH`, `BS`, `BC`. Segment i of the current segment set runs from (`GA`[6i], `GA`[6i+1], `GA`[6i+2]) to (`GA`[6i+3], `GA`[6i+4], `GA`[6i+5]) and has the label `GL`[i]; that set's hierarchy is `GO`, `GNL`, `GNH`, `GS`, `GC`. `R` holds results: `R`[0] to `R`[5] the last closest-point record, `R`[6] the best value of the last search, `R`[7] the triangle of the last signed distance, `R`[8] to `R`[15] the surface bounds, diagonal and area. `ST` is the traversal stack; it is only emptied, pushed and popped.

Sizing helpers. `zf(xs, n int)` takes an f64 growable array by reference: xs is emptied, then gets `reserve`(n), then is resized to n entries of 0.0. `zi(xs, n int, x int)` takes an i32 growable array by reference and does the same, resizing to n entries equal to x as i32. `zk(xs, n int)` takes an int growable array by reference and does the same, resizing to n entries of −1. `zu(xs, n int)` takes a u8 growable array by reference and does the same, resizing to n zeros.

`jmin(a real, b real) real` is JavaScript's Math.min of two numbers: NaN when a or b is NaN; otherwise when a equals b it is a when a is negative zero (1.0 divided by a is below 0) and b otherwise; otherwise the smaller of a and b. `jmax(a real, b real) real` is JavaScript's Math.max of two numbers: NaN when a or b is NaN; otherwise when a equals b it is b when a is negative zero and a otherwise; otherwise the larger of a and b. `hyp3(a real, b real, c real) real` is exactly V8's Math.hypot of three numbers: mx starts at 0.0; for each of a, b and c in order that is not NaN, its absolute value replaces mx when it is above mx; it returns infinity when mx equals infinity, NaN when any of a, b and c is NaN, 0.0 when mx equals 0; otherwise sum and comp start at 0.0 and, for v equal to the absolute values of a, b and c in that order, nv is v divided by mx, su is nv·nv minus comp, pre is sum plus su, comp becomes (pre minus sum) minus su and sum becomes pre; it returns sqrt(sum)·mx. `bad(x real) bool` is true when x equals 0 or x is NaN (JavaScript's falsy number). `jround(x real) real` is JavaScript's Math.round: r is floor(x); it returns r plus 1.0 when x minus r is at least 0.5, otherwise r.

`cpt(ia int, ib int, ic int, px real, py real, pz real) real` is the closest point to (px, py, pz) on the triangle whose corners start at `V`[ia], `V`[ib] and `V`[ic] (three reals each). ax, ay, az are `V`[ia], `V`[ia+1], `V`[ia+2]; abx is `V`[ib] minus ax, aby is `V`[ib+1] minus ay, abz is `V`[ib+2] minus az; acx is `V`[ic] minus ax, acy is `V`[ic+1] minus ay, acz is `V`[ic+2] minus az; apx is px minus ax, apy is py minus ay, apz is pz minus az; d1 is (abx·apx plus aby·apy) plus abz·apz; d2 is (acx·apx plus acy·apy) plus acz·apz. u and v start at 0.0 and r (an int) at 0. When d1 is at most 0 and d2 is at most 0, r becomes 4. Otherwise: bpx is apx minus abx, bpy is apy minus aby, bpz is apz minus abz; d3 is (abx·bpx plus aby·bpy) plus abz·bpz; d4 is (acx·bpx plus acy·bpy) plus acz·bpz; vc is d1·d4 minus d3·d2. When d3 is at least 0 and d4 is at most d3, u becomes 1.0 and r becomes 5. Otherwise when vc is at most 0 and d1 is at least 0 and d3 is at most 0, u becomes d1 divided by (d1 minus d3) and r becomes 1. Otherwise: cpx is apx minus acx, cpy is apy minus acy, cpz is apz minus acz; d5 is (abx·cpx plus aby·cpy) plus abz·cpz; d6 is (acx·cpx plus acy·cpy) plus acz·cpz; vb is d5·d2 minus d1·d6; va is d3·d6 minus d5·d4. When d6 is at least 0 and d5 is at most d6, v becomes 1.0 and r becomes 6. Otherwise when vb is at most 0 and d2 is at least 0 and d6 is at most 0, v becomes d2 divided by (d2 minus d6) and r becomes 2. Otherwise when va is at most 0 and d4 minus d3 is at least 0 and d5 minus d6 is at least 0: w is (d4 minus d3) divided by ((d4 minus d3) plus (d5 minus d6)); u becomes 1.0 minus w, v becomes w and r becomes 3. Otherwise dn is 1.0 divided by ((va plus vb) plus vc); u becomes vb·dn and v becomes vc·dn. Then qx is (ax plus u·abx) plus v·acx, qy is (ay plus u·aby) plus v·acy, qz is (az plus u·abz) plus v·acz; `R`[0], `R`[1], `R`[2] become qx, qy, qz; `R`[3] becomes r as a real; `R`[4] becomes u and `R`[5] becomes v. It returns ((px minus qx)·(px minus qx) plus (py minus qy)·(py minus qy)) plus (pz minus qz)·(pz minus qz).

`cseg(i int, px real, py real, pz real) real` is the closest point to (px, py, pz) on segment i of the current segment set. ax, ay, az are `GA`[6i], `GA`[6i+1], `GA`[6i+2] and bx, by, bz are `GA`[6i+3], `GA`[6i+4], `GA`[6i+5]; ex is bx minus ax, ey is by minus ay, ez is bz minus az; l2 is (ex·ex plus ey·ey) plus ez·ez; t is jmin(1.0, jmax(0.0, (((px minus ax)·ex plus (py minus ay)·ey) plus (pz minus az)·ez) divided by l2)) when l2 is above 0, otherwise 0.0 (also when l2 is NaN); qx is ax plus t·ex, qy is ay plus t·ey, qz is az plus t·ez; `R`[0], `R`[1], `R`[2] become qx, qy, qz and `R`[4] becomes t. It returns ((px minus qx)·(px minus qx) plus (py minus qy)·(py minus qy)) plus (pz minus qz)·(pz minus qz).

`rtri(ia int, ib int, ic int, ox real, oy real, oz real, dx real, dy real, dz real, eps real) real` is the ray hit distance on the triangle whose corners start at `V`[ia], `V`[ib], `V`[ic]. e1x is `V`[ib] minus `V`[ia], e1y is `V`[ib+1] minus `V`[ia+1], e1z is `V`[ib+2] minus `V`[ia+2]; e2x is `V`[ic] minus `V`[ia], e2y is `V`[ic+1] minus `V`[ia+1], e2z is `V`[ic+2] minus `V`[ia+2]; px is dy·e2z minus dz·e2y; py is dz·e2x minus dx·e2z; pz is dx·e2y minus dy·e2x; det is (e1x·px plus e1y·py) plus e1z·pz. When the absolute value of det is below 1e-300 it returns infinity. iv is 1.0 divided by det; tx is ox minus `V`[ia], ty is oy minus `V`[ia+1], tz is oz minus `V`[ia+2]; u is ((tx·px plus ty·py) plus tz·pz)·iv; when u is below the negation of eps or u is above 1.0 plus eps it returns infinity. qx is ty·e1z minus tz·e1y; qy is tz·e1x minus tx·e1z; qz is tx·e1y minus ty·e1x; v is ((dx·qx plus dy·qy) plus dz·qz)·iv; when v is below the negation of eps or u plus v is above 1.0 plus eps it returns infinity. t is ((e2x·qx plus e2y·qy) plus e2z·qz)·iv; it returns t when t is above 0, otherwise infinity.

`bnode(s int, c int, ord, nl, nh, bs, bc) int` takes the i32 growable arrays ord, bs and bc and the f64 growable arrays nl and nh by reference and appends one node to a hierarchy: q is `NN`, then `NN` grows by 1; bs[q] becomes s and bc[q] becomes the larger of c and 1; for k from 0 to 2: a starts at infinity and b at negative infinity; for i from s below s plus c, with p equal to 3·ord[i] plus k, a becomes jmin(a, `BL`[p]) and b becomes jmax(b, `BH`[p]); then nl[3q+k] becomes a and nh[3q+k] becomes b. It returns q.

`msort(ord, s int, c int, ax int)` takes an i32 growable array ord by reference and stably sorts its entries at indices s to s plus c minus 1 ascending by the key `CE`[3·x plus ax] of each entry x, as a bottom-up merge sort that uses `W` (at least as long as ord) as scratch. w starts at 1; while w is below c: lo starts at s; while lo is below s plus c: mid is the smaller of lo plus w and s plus c, hi is the smaller of lo plus 2·w and s plus c; i is lo, j is mid and k is lo; while k is below hi: when j is below hi and (i equals mid or the key of ord[j] is below the key of ord[i]), `W`[k] becomes ord[j] and j grows by 1; otherwise `W`[k] becomes ord[i] and i grows by 1; k grows by 1. Then for k from lo below hi, ord[k] becomes `W`[k]. lo grows by 2·w. After each pass w doubles.

`bvh(n int, ord, nl, nh, bs, bc)` takes the same by-reference arrays as bnode and builds the hierarchy of the n boxes whose low corners are `BL`[3i], `BL`[3i+1], `BL`[3i+2] and high corners `BH`[3i], `BH`[3i+1], `BH`[3i+2]. cap is the larger of 2 and 2·n. zi(ord, n, 0); zf(nl, 3·cap); zf(nh, 3·cap); zi(bs, cap, 0); zi(bc, cap, 0); zf(`CE`, 3·n); zi(`W`, n, 0). For i below n: ord[i] becomes i and, for k from 0 to 2, `CE`[3i+k] becomes `BL`[3i+k] plus `BH`[3i+k]. `NN` becomes 0; bnode(0, n, ord, nl, nh, bs, bc) is called; `ST` is emptied; when n is above 0, 0 is pushed onto `ST`, otherwise bc[0] becomes 0. While `ST` is not empty: q is popped from `ST`; s is bs[q] and c is bc[q]; when c is at most 4, continue. ax starts at 0 and best at −1.0; for k from 0 to 2: a starts at infinity and b at negative infinity; for i from s below s plus c, v is `CE`[3·ord[i] plus k], when v is below a, a becomes v, and when v is above b, b becomes v; then when b minus a is above best, best becomes b minus a and ax becomes k. When best is not above 0.0 (also when it is NaN), continue. msort(ord, s, c, ax); h is c divided by 2 rounded down; l is bnode(s, h, ord, nl, nh, bs, bc); bnode(s plus h, c minus h, ord, nl, nh, bs, bc) is called; bs[q] becomes l and bc[q] becomes 0; l is pushed onto `ST` and then l plus 1.

`bd(q int, nl, nh, px real, py real, pz real) real` takes the f64 growable arrays nl and nh by reference: a is jmax(jmax(nl[3q] minus px, 0.0), px minus nh[3q]); b is jmax(jmax(nl[3q+1] minus py, 0.0), py minus nh[3q+1]); c is jmax(jmax(nl[3q+2] minus pz, 0.0), pz minus nh[3q+2]); it returns (a·a plus b·b) plus c·c. `slab(q int, ox real, oy real, oz real, ix real, iy real, iz real, best real) real` is the entry distance of a ray into box q of the triangle hierarchy: a is (`NL`[3q] minus ox)·ix and b is (`NH`[3q] minus ox)·ix; t0 is jmin(a, b) and t1 is jmax(a, b); a becomes (`NL`[3q+1] minus oy)·iy and b becomes (`NH`[3q+1] minus oy)·iy; t0 becomes jmax(t0, jmin(a, b)) and t1 becomes jmin(t1, jmax(a, b)); a becomes (`NL`[3q+2] minus oz)·iz and b becomes (`NH`[3q+2] minus oz)·iz; t0 becomes jmax(jmax(t0, jmin(a, b)), 0.0) and t1 becomes jmin(jmin(t1, jmax(a, b)), best). It returns t0 when t0 is at most t1·(1.0 plus 1e-12) plus 1e-12, otherwise infinity.

`rej(t int, m int, sk int, nf bool, nx real, ny real, nz real, thr real) bool` says whether triangle t is rejected by a search filter. It returns true when m is at least 0 and `MK`[m·`NT` plus t] is 0. It returns true when t equals sk. When nf is true: dn is (`N`[3t]·nx plus `N`[3t+1]·ny) plus `N`[3t+2]·nz, and it returns true when dn is above thr is false, that is `!(dn > thr)` (so a NaN dn is rejected too). Otherwise it returns false: a triangle whose dn is above thr is kept. `tnear(px real, py real, pz real, m int, sk int, nf bool, nx real, ny real, nz real, thr real) int` is the nearest triangle of the current triangle set: best starts at infinity and bi at −1. When `NT` is 0, `R`[6] becomes best and it returns −1. `ST` is emptied and 0 is pushed. While `ST` is not empty: q is popped; when bd(q, `NL`, `NH`, px, py, pz) is at least best, continue; c is `BC`[q]; when c is not 0: for i from `BS`[q] below `BS`[q] plus c: t is `ORD`[i]; d is infinity when rej(t, m, sk, nf, nx, ny, nz, thr), otherwise cpt(3·`T`[3t], 3·`T`[3t+1], 3·`T`[3t+2], px, py, pz); when d is below best, best becomes d and bi becomes t. When c is 0: l is `BS`[q]; dl is bd(l, `NL`, `NH`, px, py, pz) and dr is bd(l plus 1, `NL`, `NH`, px, py, pz); when dl is below dr, l plus 1 is pushed and then l; otherwise l is pushed and then l plus 1. After the loop `R`[6] becomes best; when bi is at least 0, cpt(3·`T`[3·bi], 3·`T`[3·bi+1], 3·`T`[3·bi+2], px, py, pz) is called once more and its result dropped. It returns bi.

`tray(ox real, oy real, oz real, dx real, dy real, dz real, tmax real, m int, sk int, eps real) int` is the first ray hit in the current triangle set: best starts at tmax and bi at −1. When `NT` is 0, `R`[6] becomes best and it returns −1. ix is 1.0 divided by (dx when the absolute value of dx is above 1e-15, otherwise 1e-15); iy and iz likewise from dy and dz. `ST` is emptied and 0 is pushed. While `ST` is not empty: q is popped; when slab(q, ox, oy, oz, ix, iy, iz, best) equals infinity, continue; c is `BC`[q]; when c is not 0: for i from `BS`[q] below `BS`[q] plus c: t is `ORD`[i]; d is infinity when rej(t, m, sk, false, 0.0, 0.0, 0.0, 0.0), otherwise rtri(3·`T`[3t], 3·`T`[3t+1], 3·`T`[3t+2], ox, oy, oz, dx, dy, dz, eps); when d is below best, best becomes d and bi becomes t. When c is 0, `BS`[q] is pushed and then `BS`[q] plus 1. After the loop `R`[6] becomes best and it returns bi.

`gnear(px real, py real, pz real, lab int, lf bool) int` is the nearest segment of the current segment set: best starts at infinity and bi at −1. When `NG` is 0, `R`[6] becomes best and it returns −1. `ST` is emptied and 0 is pushed. While `ST` is not empty: q is popped; when bd(q, `GNL`, `GNH`, px, py, pz) is at least best, continue; c is `GC`[q]; when c is not 0: for i from `GS`[q] below `GS`[q] plus c: g is `GO`[i]; d is infinity when lf is true and `GL`[g] is not lab, otherwise cseg(g, px, py, pz); when d is below best, best becomes d and bi becomes g. When c is 0: l is `GS`[q]; dl is bd(l, `GNL`, `GNH`, px, py, pz) and dr is bd(l plus 1, `GNL`, `GNH`, px, py, pz); when dl is below dr, l plus 1 is pushed and then l; otherwise l is pushed and then l plus 1. After the loop `R`[6] becomes best; when bi is at least 0, cseg(bi, px, py, pz) is called once more and its result dropped. It returns bi.

`sdf1(px real, py real, pz real) real` is the signed distance to the current surface: t is tnear(px, py, pz, −1, −1, false, 0.0, 0.0, 0.0, 0.0). When t is below 0, `R`[7] becomes −1.0 and it returns infinity. Otherwise `R`[7] becomes t as a real; r is `R`[3] as an int; nx, ny, nz are `N`[3t], `N`[3t+1], `N`[3t+2]. When r is at least 4: v is 3·`T`[3t plus r minus 4]; nx, ny, nz become `VN`[v], `VN`[v+1], `VN`[v+2]. Otherwise when r is at least 1: k is 0 when r is 1, 2 when r is 2 and 1 otherwise; u is `TT`[3t plus k]; when u is at least 0, nx becomes nx plus `N`[3u], ny becomes ny plus `N`[3u+1] and nz becomes nz plus `N`[3u+2]. ex is px minus `R`[0], ey is py minus `R`[1], ez is pz minus `R`[2]; d is hyp3(ex, ey, ez); it returns d when (ex·nx plus ey·ny) plus ez·nz is above 0.0, otherwise the negation of d.

`keq(a real, b real) bool` is true when a equals b or both a and b are NaN. `lo32(x real) int` is (x truncated toward zero as an int when the absolute value of x is below 1e15, otherwise 0) and 4294967295. `hk(x real, y real, z real) int`: m is ((lo32(x)·73856093) xor (lo32(y)·19349663)) xor (lo32(z)·83492791); it returns m xor (m shifted right 21). `he(key int) int`: h is (key and 4294967295) xor (key shifted right 32); it returns (h·40503) xor (h shifted right 15).

`weld(np int, q real) int` merges the np input points of `P` that round to the same grid cell of size q, keeping first-seen order. hs starts at 16 and doubles while it is below 2·np. zi(`HT`, hs, −1); `KX`, `KY` and `KZ` are each emptied and then get `reserve`(np); `V` is emptied and then gets `reserve`(3·np); zi(`VM`, np, 0). nv starts at 0. For i below np: rx is jround(`P`[3i] divided by q), ry is jround(`P`[3i+1] divided by q), rz is jround(`P`[3i+2] divided by q); s is hk(rx, ry, rz) and (hs minus 1); j is −1; while `HT`[s] is not −1 and j is −1: e is `HT`[s]; when keq(`KX`[e], rx) and keq(`KY`[e], ry) and keq(`KZ`[e], rz), j becomes e; otherwise s becomes (s plus 1) and (hs minus 1). When j is still −1: j becomes nv; `HT`[s] becomes nv; rx, ry, rz are pushed onto `KX`, `KY`, `KZ`; `P`[3i], `P`[3i+1], `P`[3i+2] are pushed onto `V` in that order; nv grows by 1. Then `VM`[i] becomes j. It returns nv.

`tris(nt int, np int)` fills the triangles. zi(`T`, 3·nt, 0); for i below 3·nt: x is `I`[i]; `T`[i] becomes `VM`[x] when x is at least 0 and below np, otherwise 0. zf(`N`, 3·nt); zf(`AR`, nt); zf(`C`, 3·nt); zf(`BL`, 3·nt); zf(`BH`, 3·nt). For t below nt: a is 3·`T`[3t], b is 3·`T`[3t+1], c is 3·`T`[3t+2]; ux is `V`[b] minus `V`[a], uy is `V`[b+1] minus `V`[a+1], uz is `V`[b+2] minus `V`[a+2]; wx is `V`[c] minus `V`[a], wy is `V`[c+1] minus `V`[a+1], wz is `V`[c+2] minus `V`[a+2]; nx is uy·wz minus uz·wy; ny is uz·wx minus ux·wz; nz is ux·wy minus uy·wx; l is hyp3(nx, ny, nz); `AR`[t] becomes l divided by 2.0; `N`[3t] becomes 0.0 when bad(l), otherwise nx divided by l; `N`[3t+1] becomes 0.0 when bad(l), otherwise ny divided by l; `N`[3t+2] becomes 0.0 when bad(l), otherwise nz divided by l; for k from 0 to 2: `C`[3t+k] becomes ((`V`[a+k] plus `V`[b+k]) plus `V`[c+k]) divided by 3.0, `BL`[3t+k] becomes jmin(jmin(`V`[a+k], `V`[b+k]), `V`[c+k]) and `BH`[3t+k] becomes jmax(jmax(`V`[a+k], `V`[b+k]), `V`[c+k]).

`adj(nt int)` pairs triangles across shared edges. zi(`TT`, 3·nt, −1). es starts at 16 and doubles while it is below 6·nt. zk(`EK`, es); zi(`EV`, es, 0). For t below nt, for k from 0 to 2: a is `T`[3t+k] and b is `T`[3t plus (k plus 1) mod 3] (as ints); when a equals b, continue; key is (the smaller of a and b)·67108864 plus the larger of a and b; s is he(key) and (es minus 1); while `EK`[s] is not −1 and `EK`[s] is not key, s becomes (s plus 1) and (es minus 1). When `EK`[s] is −1: `EK`[s] becomes key and `EV`[s] becomes 3t plus k. Otherwise o is `EV`[s]; when o is at least 0: `TT`[3t+k] becomes o divided by 3 rounded down, `TT`[o] becomes t and `EV`[s] becomes −1.

`vnorm(nt int, nv int)` accumulates angle-weighted vertex normals. zf(`VN`, 3·nv). For t below nt, for k from 0 to 2: a is 3·`T`[3t+k], b is 3·`T`[3t plus (k plus 1) mod 3], c is 3·`T`[3t plus (k plus 2) mod 3]; ux is `V`[b] minus `V`[a], uy is `V`[b+1] minus `V`[a+1], uz is `V`[b+2] minus `V`[a+2]; wx is `V`[c] minus `V`[a], wy is `V`[c+1] minus `V`[a+1], wz is `V`[c+2] minus `V`[a+2]; dn is hyp3(ux, uy, uz)·hyp3(wx, wy, wz), replaced by 1.0 when bad(dn); an is ms_acos(jmax(−1.0, jmin(1.0, ((ux·wx plus uy·wy) plus uz·wz) divided by dn))); for j from 0 to 2, `VN`[a+j] becomes `VN`[a+j] plus an·`N`[3t+j].

`full(nt int)` sizes the per-triangle query arrays of a full surface: zu(`MK`, 7·nt); zf(`HS`, nt); zf(`TH`, nt); zi(`TJ`, nt, 0); zf(`TC`, nt). Then it reports `ms_view_u8(23, MK)`, `ms_view_f64(24, HS)`, `ms_view_f64(25, TH)`, `ms_view_i32(26, TJ)` and `ms_view_f64(27, TC)`.

The exported function `ms_init()` does zf(`R`, 16), empties `ST` and gives it `reserve`(256), and reports `ms_view_f64(15, R)`. The exported function `ms_bin(np i32, nt i32)` does zf(`P`, 3·np) and zi(`I`, 3·nt, 0) and reports `ms_view_f64(1, P)` and `ms_view_i32(2, I)`.

The exported function `ms_surf(np i32, nt i32) i32` builds a full surface from the np points in `P` and the nt triangles in `I` (ints from here on) and makes it the current triangle set. lo0, lo1, lo2 start at infinity and hi0, hi1, hi2 at negative infinity; for i below np, for k from 0 to 2: lo_k becomes jmin(lo_k, `P`[3i+k]) and hi_k becomes jmax(hi_k, `P`[3i+k]). diag is hyp3(hi0 minus lo0, hi1 minus lo1, hi2 minus lo2), replaced by 1.0 when bad(diag). nv is weld(np, diag·1e-7). Then tris(nt, np), adj(nt) and vnorm(nt, nv) are called, then bvh(nt, `ORD`, `NL`, `NH`, `BS`, `BC`), then full(nt); `NT` becomes nt. area starts at 0.0 and adds `AR`[t] for t below nt in order. `R`[8], `R`[9], `R`[10] become lo0, lo1, lo2; `R`[11], `R`[12], `R`[13] become hi0, hi1, hi2; `R`[14] becomes diag and `R`[15] becomes area. It reports `ms_view_f64(3, V)`, `ms_view_i32(4, T)`, `ms_view_f64(5, N)`, `ms_view_f64(6, AR)`, `ms_view_f64(7, C)`, `ms_view_i32(8, TT)`, `ms_view_f64(9, VN)`, `ms_view_i32(10, ORD)`, `ms_view_f64(11, NL)`, `ms_view_f64(12, NH)`, `ms_view_i32(13, BS)` and `ms_view_i32(14, BC)`, and returns nv.

The exported function `ms_tl(nv i32, nt i32, fl i32)` sizes the current triangle set for loading from JavaScript: cap is the larger of 2 and 2·nt; zf(`V`, 3·nv); zi(`T`, 3·nt, 0); zi(`ORD`, nt, 0); zf(`NL`, 3·cap); zf(`NH`, 3·cap); zi(`BS`, cap, 0); zi(`BC`, cap, 0); `NT` becomes nt. When fl is not 0 it also does zf(`N`, 3·nt), zf(`C`, 3·nt), zi(`TT`, 3·nt, −1), zf(`VN`, 3·nv) and full(nt), and reports `ms_view_f64(5, N)`, `ms_view_f64(7, C)`, `ms_view_i32(8, TT)` and `ms_view_f64(9, VN)`. It always reports `ms_view_f64(3, V)`, `ms_view_i32(4, T)`, `ms_view_i32(10, ORD)`, `ms_view_f64(11, NL)`, `ms_view_f64(12, NH)`, `ms_view_i32(13, BS)` and `ms_view_i32(14, BC)`. The exported function `ms_tbvh(nt i32)` builds the hierarchy of the loaded triangle set: zf(`BL`, 3·nt) and zf(`BH`, 3·nt); for t below nt, for k from 0 to 2, `BL`[3t+k] becomes jmin(jmin(`V`[3·`T`[3t] plus k], `V`[3·`T`[3t+1] plus k]), `V`[3·`T`[3t+2] plus k]) and `BH`[3t+k] becomes jmax(jmax(`V`[3·`T`[3t] plus k], `V`[3·`T`[3t+1] plus k]), `V`[3·`T`[3t+2] plus k]); then bvh(nt, `ORD`, `NL`, `NH`, `BS`, `BC`); `NT` becomes nt; it reports views 10 to 14 as `ms_tl` does.

The exported function `ms_gl(ns i32)` sizes the current segment set: gcap is the larger of 2 and 2·ns; zf(`GA`, 6·ns); zi(`GL`, ns, 0); zi(`GO`, ns, 0); zf(`GNL`, 3·gcap); zf(`GNH`, 3·gcap); zi(`GS`, gcap, 0); zi(`GC`, gcap, 0); `NG` becomes ns. It reports `ms_view_f64(16, GA)`, `ms_view_i32(17, GL)`, `ms_view_i32(18, GO)`, `ms_view_f64(19, GNL)`, `ms_view_f64(20, GNH)`, `ms_view_i32(21, GS)` and `ms_view_i32(22, GC)`. The exported function `ms_gbvh(ns i32)` builds the hierarchy of the loaded segments: zf(`BL`, 3·ns) and zf(`BH`, 3·ns); for i below ns, for k from 0 to 2, `BL`[3i+k] becomes jmin(`GA`[6i+k], `GA`[6i+3+k]) and `BH`[3i+k] becomes jmax(`GA`[6i+k], `GA`[6i+3+k]); then bvh(ns, `GO`, `GNL`, `GNH`, `GS`, `GC`); `NG` becomes ns; it reports views 18 to 22 as `ms_gl` does.

The exported function `ms_cl(px real, py real, pz real, m i32, sk i32, nx real, ny real, nz real, thr real, nf i32) i32` returns tnear(px, py, pz, m, sk, nf is not 0, nx, ny, nz, thr). The exported function `ms_ray(ox real, oy real, oz real, dx real, dy real, dz real, tmax real, m i32, sk i32, eps real) i32` returns tray(ox, oy, oz, dx, dy, dz, tmax, m, sk, eps). The exported function `ms_sdf(px real, py real, pz real) real` returns sdf1(px, py, pz). The exported function `ms_gn(px real, py real, pz real, lab i32, lf i32) i32` returns gnear(px, py, pz, lab, lf is not 0).

The exported function `ms_thick(diag real)` measures the wall thickness behind every triangle of the current full surface: eps is diag·1e-7; for t below `NT`: ox is `C`[3t] minus `N`[3t]·eps, oy is `C`[3t+1] minus `N`[3t+1]·eps, oz is `C`[3t+2] minus `N`[3t+2]·eps; j is tray(ox, oy, oz, the negation of `N`[3t], the negation of `N`[3t+1], the negation of `N`[3t+2], diag·2.0, −1, t, 1e-9); `TH`[t] becomes `R`[6] plus eps when j is at least 0, otherwise infinity; `TJ`[t] becomes j; `TC`[t] becomes (`N`[3t]·`N`[3j] plus `N`[3t+1]·`N`[3j+1]) plus `N`[3t+2]·`N`[3j+2] when j is at least 0, otherwise 0.0.

The exported function `ms_cls(cx real, cy real, cz real, r real) real` classifies an octree cell with center (cx, cy, cz) and radius r against the current full surface, with the target sizes in `HS`. lx is cx minus r, ly is cy minus r, lz is cz minus r; hx is cx plus r, hy is cy plus r, hz is cz plus r; rr is r·r; need starts at infinity and any at false. When `NT` is above 0: `ST` is emptied and 0 is pushed; while `ST` is not empty: q is popped; when `NL`[3q] is above hx or `NH`[3q] is below lx or `NL`[3q+1] is above hy or `NH`[3q+1] is below ly or `NL`[3q+2] is above hz or `NH`[3q+2] is below lz, continue; c is `BC`[q]; when c is not 0: for i from `BS`[q] below `BS`[q] plus c: t is `ORD`[i]; when (`HS`[t] is below need or any is false) and cpt(3·`T`[3t], 3·`T`[3t+1], 3·`T`[3t+2], cx, cy, cz) is at most rr, any becomes true and need becomes jmin(need, `HS`[t]). When c is 0, `BS`[q] is pushed and then `BS`[q] plus 1. When any is true it returns need. Otherwise it returns 0.0 when sdf1(cx, cy, cz) is above 0.0, and −1.0 otherwise.

Growth helpers for the octree part. `QF` (real), `QI` (i32), `QB` (u8) and `QL` (int) are scratch growable arrays used only by these helpers. `cw(xs, m int)` exists for real, i32, u8 and int growable arrays taken by reference and makes xs.cap at least m without changing its entries: when m is above xs.cap: the scratch of the same element type is emptied and then gets `reserve`(the length of xs), then gets a copy of xs with one `append`; xs is emptied and then gets `reserve`(m), then gets the scratch back with one `append`; the scratch is emptied. `pf(xs, x real)` takes a real growable array by reference, `pi(xs, x int)` an i32 one, `pb(xs, x int)` a u8 one and `pk(xs, x int)` an int one: when the length of xs is at least xs.cap, cw(xs, 2·(the length of xs) plus 64) is called; then x (as i32 for pi, as u8 for pb) is pushed onto xs. Every octree array below grows only through pf, pi, pb or pk, or is sized with zf, zi, zk or zu.

Hash maps from non-negative int keys to non-negative values are kept as an int growable array ks of keys and an i32 growable array vs of values (both taken by reference) of the same power-of-two length; a key slot holds −1 when empty and −2 when its entry was deleted. `mnew(ks, vs, m int)` does zk(ks, m) and zi(vs, m, 0). `mfind(ks, key int) int` returns −1 when key is below 0; otherwise m is the length of ks and s is he(key) and (m minus 1); while ks[s] is not −1: when ks[s] equals key it returns s; s becomes (s plus 1) and (m minus 1). Then it returns −1. `mget(ks, vs, key int) int`: s is mfind(ks, key); it returns vs[s] as an int when s is at least 0, otherwise −1. `mdel(ks, key int)`: s is mfind(ks, key); when s is at least 0, ks[s] becomes −2. `mins(ks, vs, key int, v int) int` stores key with value v in a table that still has an empty slot: m is the length of ks, s is he(key) and (m minus 1) and ft is −1; while ks[s] is not −1: when ks[s] equals key, vs[s] becomes v as i32 and it returns 0; when ks[s] is −2 and ft is −1, ft becomes s; s becomes (s plus 1) and (m minus 1). When ft is at least 0, ks[ft] becomes key, vs[ft] becomes v as i32 and it returns 0; otherwise ks[s] becomes key, vs[s] becomes v as i32 and it returns 1. `mre(ks, vs) int` doubles the table: m is the length of ks; `QK` (int) is emptied and then gets `reserve`(m); `QV` (i32) is emptied and then gets `reserve`(m); for s below m, when ks[s] is at least 0, ks[s] is pushed onto `QK` and vs[s] onto `QV`; then zk(ks, 2·m) and zi(vs, 2·m, 0); for e below the length of `QK`, mins(ks, vs, `QK`[e], `QV`[e] as int) is called; it returns the length of `QK`. `mput(ks, vs, key int, v int, u int) int` stores key with value v where u counts the non-empty slots, and returns the new count: when 2·(u plus 1) is above the length of ks, u becomes mre(ks, vs); it returns u plus mins(ks, vs, key, v).

Hex tables. `hxn(a int, k int) int` is the offset along axis k (0 for x, 1 for y, 2 for z) of corner a of the unit hex, which is 1 or −1: along x it is 1 for the corners 1, 2, 5 and 6; along y it is 1 for the corners 2, 3, 6 and 7; along z it is 1 for the corners 4, 5, 6 and 7; otherwise it is −1. `hfc(f int, i int) int` is entry i (0 to 3) of face f (0 to 5) from the rows 0: 0, 3, 2, 1; 1: 4, 5, 6, 7; 2: 0, 1, 5, 4; 3: 1, 2, 6, 5; 4: 2, 3, 7, 6; 5: 3, 0, 4, 7. `dirk(f int, k int) int` is component k of the outward direction of face f from the rows 0: (0, 0, −1); 1: (0, 0, 1); 2: (0, −1, 0); 3: (1, 0, 0); 4: (0, 1, 0); 5: (−1, 0, 0). `hed(g int, s int) int` is end s (0 or 1) of hex edge g (0 to 11) from the pairs 0: (0, 1); 1: (1, 2); 2: (2, 3); 3: (3, 0); 4: (4, 5); 5: (5, 6); 6: (6, 7); 7: (7, 4); 8: (0, 4); 9: (1, 5); 10: (2, 6); 11: (3, 7).

Octree state, all starting at 0 or empty. Ints: `OL` (finest level), `OGR` (grid size), `OQ` (cells), `OIU`, `ONU`, `OSU` (non-empty slot counts of the three maps), `ONL` (leaves), `ON0` (grid nodes), `ONT` (boundary nodes), `OT0`, `ONC` (core leaves), `ONQ` (boundary quads), `ONB`, `ORF`, `ORY`, `OFE`, `OCO`, `OHD`, `ONN`, `ONE`, `OBG` (the cell budget; 0 means no budget), `OBT` (the cell target behind it), `OFX` (1 when the base size is fixed). Reals: `OFS` and `OFH` (the factors the budget applied to stc and hc, both starting at 1.0), `OX`, `OY`, `OZ` (grid origin), `OEX` (grid extent), `OHC` (base cell size), `OHM` (smallest target size). Growable arrays: i32 `LV`, `CI`, `CJ`, `CK` (cell level and integer position), u8 `AL` (cell alive), real `BDV` (cell class); the cell map `IK` (int keys) and `IV` (i32 values); the node map `NMK` (int) and `NMV` (i32) with int `NKK` (the key of each node); the distance cache map `SDK` (int) and `SDI` (i32) with real `SDV`; real `XP`, `SD`, `NB`, `NW`, `HBV`, `TX`, `TX0`, `DS`, `XO`, `CU`, `HW`, `HWO`, `ORS`, `TRR`, `TRA`, `TRT`; i32 `LF`, `LI`, `E0`, `OS`, `WK`, `NBR`, `NCL`, `SP`, `CEL`, `HN`, `HC`, `HM`, `HX`, `TE`, `BQ`, `BP`, `BO`, `BJ`, `BN`, `TP`, `TLB`, `PT`, `CO`, `US`, `EL`, `BF`, `HNO`, `HMO`, `TRO`, `TRW`; u8 `CR`, `CM`, `UZ`, `HA`, `IB`, `TF`, `FMK`.

`hzf(l int) real` is `OEX` divided by ((1 shifted left by l) as a real). `okey(l int, i int, j int, k int) int` is ((l·4096 plus i)·4096 plus j)·4096 plus k. `onk(i int, j int, k int) int` is (i·(`OGR` plus 1) plus j)·(`OGR` plus 1) plus k. `ocls(l int, i int, j int, k int) real` classifies a cell: h is hzf(l); cx is `OX` plus ((i as a real) plus 0.5)·h; cy is `OY` plus ((j as a real) plus 0.5)·h; cz is `OZ` plus ((k as a real) plus 0.5)·h; r is (h·0.8660254)·1.0001; it returns ms_cls(cx, cy, cz, r). `oadd(l int, i int, j int, k int, c real) int` appends a cell: q is `OQ`; pi(`LV`, l), pi(`CI`, i), pi(`CJ`, j), pi(`CK`, k), pf(`BDV`, c) and pb(`AL`, 1); `OQ` grows by 1; `OIU` becomes mput(`IK`, `IV`, okey(l, i, j, k), q, `OIU`); it returns q. `oup(l int, i int, j int, k int, m0 int) int` finds the coarser cell holding a position: for m from m0 down to 0: s is l minus m; q is mget(`IK`, `IV`, okey(m, i shifted right by s, j shifted right by s, k shifted right by s)); when q is at least 0 it returns q. Then it returns −1.

`osplit(q int, out)` takes an i32 growable array out by reference and splits cell q: `AL`[q] becomes 0; mdel(`IK`, okey(`LV`[q], `CI`[q], `CJ`[q], `CK`[q])); l is `LV`[q] plus 1; for a from 0 to 7: i is 2·`CI`[q] plus (a and 1), j is 2·`CJ`[q] plus ((a shifted right by 1) and 1), k is 2·`CK`[q] plus (a shifted right by 2); c is ocls(l, i, j, k); when c is not 0.0, pi(out, oadd(l, i, j, k, c)) is called. `obal()` balances the cells on the work stack `WK`: while `WK` is not empty: q is popped from `WK`; when `AL`[q] is 0 or `LV`[q] is below 2, continue; l is `LV`[q] and n is 1 shifted left by l; hit starts false; for d from 0 to 26 while hit is false: when d is 13, skip it; i is `CI`[q] plus (d mod 3) minus 1, j is `CJ`[q] plus ((d divided by 3, rounded down) mod 3) minus 1, k is `CK`[q] plus (d divided by 9, rounded down) minus 1; when any of i, j, k is below 0 or at least n, skip it; p is oup(l, i, j, k, l minus 2); when p is at least 0: osplit(p, `WK`) is called, then pi(`WK`, q), and hit becomes true.

`onbr(q int, f int) int` finds the neighbors of cell q across its face f: `NBR` is emptied; dx, dy, dz are dirk(f, 0), dirk(f, 1), dirk(f, 2); l is `LV`[q] and n is 1 shifted left by l; i is `CI`[q] plus dx, j is `CJ`[q] plus dy, k is `CK`[q] plus dz. When any of i, j, k is below 0 or at least n it returns 0. p is mget(`IK`, `IV`, okey(l, i, j, k)); when p is at least 0: pi(`NBR`, p) and it returns 0. p becomes oup(l, i, j, k, l minus 1); when p is at least 0: pi(`NBR`, p) and it returns −1. For a from 0 to 7: ii is 2·i plus (a and 1), jj is 2·j plus ((a shifted right by 1) and 1), kk is 2·k plus (a shifted right by 2); the child is skipped when (dx is not 0 and (ii and 1) equals (1 when dx is above 0, otherwise 0)) or (dy is not 0 and (jj and 1) equals (1 when dy is above 0, otherwise 0)) or (dz is not 0 and (kk and 1) equals (1 when dz is above 0, otherwise 0)); otherwise c is mget(`IK`, `IV`, okey(l plus 1, ii, jj, kk)) and when c is at least 0, pi(`NBR`, c). It returns 1. `oisc(q int) bool` is true when q is at least 0 and `LI`[q] is at least 0 and `CR`[`LI`[q]] is 1.

`onid(i int, j int, k int) int` returns the node at grid position (i, j, k), creating it when new: kk is onk(i, j, k); n is mget(`NMK`, `NMV`, kk); when n is below 0: n becomes the length of `XP` divided by 3; `ONU` becomes mput(`NMK`, `NMV`, kk, n, `ONU`); pk(`NKK`, kk); pf(`XP`, `OX` plus ((i as a real)·`OEX`) divided by (`OGR` as a real)); pf(`XP`, `OY` plus ((j as a real)·`OEX`) divided by (`OGR` as a real)); pf(`XP`, `OZ` plus ((k as a real)·`OEX`) divided by (`OGR` as a real)). It returns n.

`onodes(dl real)` rebuilds the leaves, their nodes and the core flags: `LF` is emptied and `ONB` becomes 0; for q below `OQ`: when `AL`[q] is not 0: pi(`LF`, q), and when `BDV`[q] is above 0.0, `ONB` grows by 1. `ONL` is the length of `LF`. mnew(`NMK`, `NMV`, 1024); `ONU` becomes 0; `XP` and `NKK` are emptied. zi(`E0`, 8·`ONL`, 0); for e below `ONL`: q is `LF`[e]; s is `OL` minus `LV`[q]; z is 1 shifted left by s; for a from 0 to 7: `E0`[8e+a] becomes onid((`CI`[q] shifted left by s) plus (z when hxn(a, 0) is above 0, otherwise 0), (`CJ`[q] shifted left by s) plus (z when hxn(a, 1) is above 0, otherwise 0), (`CK`[q] shifted left by s) plus (z when hxn(a, 2) is above 0, otherwise 0)) as i32. `ON0` is the length of `XP` divided by 3. zf(`SD`, `ON0`); for n below `ON0`: kk is `NKK`[n]; x is mget(`SDK`, `SDI`, kk); when x is below 0: x becomes the length of `SDV`; pf(`SDV`, sdf1(`XP`[3n], `XP`[3n+1], `XP`[3n+2])); `OSU` becomes mput(`SDK`, `SDI`, kk, x, `OSU`). `SD`[n] becomes `SDV`[x]. zu(`CR`, `ONL`); for e below `ONL`: h is hzf(`LV`[`LF`[e]]); c is 1; for a from 0 to 7 while c is 1: when `SD`[`E0`[8e+a]] is below (the negation of dl)·h is false (also when NaN), c becomes 0; `CR`[e] becomes c. zi(`LI`, `OQ`, −1); for e below `ONL`, `LI`[`LF`[e]] becomes e.

`orefine() bool` splits core leaves that touch fewer than four core neighbors across a face: `WK` and `SP` are emptied; for e below `ONL`: when `CR`[e] is 0, continue; q is `LF`[e]; for f from 0 to 5: when onbr(q, f) is not 1, continue to the next f; nc counts the entries x of `NBR` with oisc(x); when nc is above 0 and below 4: pi(`SP`, q) and stop the f loop. When `SP` is empty it returns false. `ORF` grows by the length of `SP`; for each q of `SP` in order, osplit(q, `WK`); then obal() and it returns true. `ocore()` peels core leaves: ch starts true and it at 0; while ch is true and it is below 20: ch becomes false; for e below `ONL`: when `CR`[e] is 0, continue; q is `LF`[e]; for f from 0 to 5: when onbr(q, f) is not 1, continue to the next f; `NCL` is emptied and every entry x of `NBR` with oisc(x) is pushed onto it in order with pi; when `NCL` is not empty and its length is below 4: for each x of `NCL`, `CR`[`LI`[x]] becomes 0, and ch becomes true. Then it grows by 1. After the loop `CEL` is emptied and every e below `ONL` with `CR`[e] equal to 1 is pushed onto it in order with pi; `ONC` is the length of `CEL`; zu(`CM`, `ONL`) and `CM`[e] becomes `CR`[e] for every e below `ONL`.

`ohang()` records hanging nodes: `HN`, `HC`, `HM` and `HW` are emptied; zi(`HX`, `ON0`, −1). For e below `ONL`: q is `LF`[e]; when `CM`[e] is 0 or `LV`[q] is at least `OL`, continue; s is `OL` minus `LV`[q]; z is 1 shifted left by s; hz is z shifted right by 1; i0 is `CI`[q] shifted left by s, j0 is `CJ`[q] shifted left by s, k0 is `CK`[q] shifted left by s. For g from 0 to 11: a is hed(g, 0) and b is hed(g, 1); n is mget(`NMK`, `NMV`, onk(i0 plus ((hxn(a, 0) plus hxn(b, 0)) plus 2)·hz divided by 2, j0 plus ((hxn(a, 1) plus hxn(b, 1)) plus 2)·hz divided by 2, k0 plus ((hxn(a, 2) plus hxn(b, 2)) plus 2)·hz divided by 2)) (the product is divided by 2); when n is at least 0 and `HX`[n] is −1: `HX`[n] becomes the length of `HN`; pi(`HN`, n); pi(`HC`, 2); pi(`HM`, `E0`[8e+a]), pi(`HM`, `E0`[8e+b]), pi(`HM`, −1), pi(`HM`, −1); pf(`HW`, 0.5), pf(`HW`, 0.5), pf(`HW`, 0.0), pf(`HW`, 0.0). Then for f from 0 to 5: sx, sy, sz are the sums of hxn(hfc(f, i), 0), hxn(hfc(f, i), 1) and hxn(hfc(f, i), 2) over i from 0 to 3; n is mget(`NMK`, `NMV`, onk(i0 plus (sx divided by 4 plus 1)·hz, j0 plus (sy divided by 4 plus 1)·hz, k0 plus (sz divided by 4 plus 1)·hz)); when n is at least 0 and `HX`[n] is −1: `HX`[n] becomes the length of `HN`; pi(`HN`, n); pi(`HC`, 4); pi(`HM`, `E0`[8e plus hfc(f, i)]) for i from 0 to 3; pf(`HW`, 0.25) four times. Afterwards zu(`UZ`, `ON0`); for e below `ONL` with `CM`[e] not 0, `UZ`[`E0`[8e+a]] becomes 1 for a from 0 to 7. zu(`HA`, the length of `HN`); for x below the length of `HN`: when `UZ`[`HN`[x]] is 1, `HA`[x] becomes 1; otherwise `HX`[`HN`[x]] becomes −1.

`obq()` collects the boundary quads of the core: `BQ` is emptied; zf(`NB`, 3·`ON0`); zu(`IB`, `ON0`). For each e of `CEL` in order: q is `LF`[e]; for f from 0 to 5: onbr(q, f) is called; when some entry x of `NBR` has oisc(x), continue to the next f. Otherwise n0, n1, n2, n3 are `E0`[8e plus hfc(f, i)] for i from 0 to 3; pi(`BQ`, n0), pi(`BQ`, n1), pi(`BQ`, n2), pi(`BQ`, n3), pi(`BQ`, e); ux is `XP`[3·n2] minus `XP`[3·n0], uy is `XP`[3·n2+1] minus `XP`[3·n0+1], uz is `XP`[3·n2+2] minus `XP`[3·n0+2]; wx is `XP`[3·n3] minus `XP`[3·n1], wy is `XP`[3·n3+1] minus `XP`[3·n1+1], wz is `XP`[3·n3+2] minus `XP`[3·n1+2]; nx is uy·wz minus uz·wy; ny is uz·wx minus ux·wz; nz is ux·wy minus uy·wx; for each n of n0, n1, n2, n3 in that order: `NB`[3n] becomes `NB`[3n] plus nx, `NB`[3n+1] becomes `NB`[3n+1] plus ny, `NB`[3n+2] becomes `NB`[3n+2] plus nz, and `IB`[n] becomes 1. `ONQ` is the length of `BQ` divided by 5.

`oadj(nsm int)` smooths the boundary normals over the quad edges: `BP` is emptied; for b below `ONQ`, for k from 0 to 3: a is `BQ`[5b+k] and c is `BQ`[5b plus (k plus 1) mod 4]; pi(`BP`, a), pi(`BP`, c), pi(`BP`, c), pi(`BP`, a). zi(`BO`, `ON0` plus 1, 0); for each pair (u, v) of `BP` (entries 2p and 2p+1), `BO`[u plus 1] grows by 1; then for n from 1 to `ON0`, `BO`[n] grows by `BO`[n minus 1]. zi(`BJ`, the length of `BP` divided by 2, 0); zi(`BN`, `ON0`, 0); for each pair (u, v) of `BP` in order: s is `BO`[u]; when v is not among `BJ`[s] to `BJ`[s plus `BN`[u] minus 1], `BJ`[s plus `BN`[u]] becomes v and `BN`[u] grows by 1. For n below `ON0` with `IB`[n] not 0: l is hyp3(`NB`[3n], `NB`[3n+1], `NB`[3n+2]), replaced by 1.0 when bad(l); `NB`[3n], `NB`[3n+1] and `NB`[3n+2] are each divided by l. Then nsm times: zf(`NW`, 3·`ON0`); for n below `ON0` with `IB`[n] not 0: x is `NB`[3n], y is `NB`[3n+1], z is `NB`[3n+2]; for each m of `BJ`[`BO`[n]] to `BJ`[`BO`[n] plus `BN`[n] minus 1] in order: x becomes x plus `NB`[3m], y becomes y plus `NB`[3m+1], z becomes z plus `NB`[3m+2]; l is hyp3(x, y, z), replaced by 1.0 when bad(l); `NW`[3n] becomes x divided by l, `NW`[3n+1] becomes y divided by l, `NW`[3n+2] becomes z divided by l. Then for n below `ON0` with `IB`[n] not 0, `NB`[3n], `NB`[3n+1], `NB`[3n+2] become `NW`[3n], `NW`[3n+1], `NW`[3n+2].

`oproj()` projects the boundary nodes onto the surface: zi(`TLB`, `ON0`, −1); zi(`TP`, `ON0`, −1); zu(`TF`, `ON0`); zf(`HBV`, `ON0`); `TX` and `TE` are emptied; `ONT` and `ORY` become 0. For each e of `CEL`: h is hzf(`LV`[`LF`[e]]); for a from 0 to 7: n is `E0`[8e+a] and `HBV`[n] becomes jmax(`HBV`[n], h). For n below `ON0`: when `IB`[n] is 0, continue; `TP`[n] becomes `ONT` and `ONT` grows by 1; x is `HX`[n]; when x is at least 0 and `IB`[`HM`[4x plus i]] is 1 for every i below `HC`[x]: pf(`TX`, 0.0) three times, pi(`TE`, x), `TF`[n] becomes 1, and continue. px is `XP`[3n], py is `XP`[3n+1], pz is `XP`[3n+2]; l is hyp3(`NB`[3n], `NB`[3n+1], `NB`[3n+2]), replaced by 1.0 when bad(l); dx is `NB`[3n] divided by l, dy is `NB`[3n+1] divided by l, dz is `NB`[3n+2] divided by l; j is tray(px, py, pz, dx, dy, dz, 4.0·`HBV`[n], −1, −1, 1e-9); when j is at least 0: qx is px plus `R`[6]·dx, qy is py plus `R`[6]·dy, qz is pz plus `R`[6]·dz; otherwise j becomes tnear(px, py, pz, −1, −1, false, 0.0, 0.0, 0.0, 0.0), qx, qy, qz are `R`[0], `R`[1], `R`[2] and `ORY` grows by 1. pf(`TX`, qx), pf(`TX`, qy), pf(`TX`, qz); `TLB`[`TP`[n]] becomes `PT`[j] when j is at least 0, otherwise −1.

`ofd(i int) real` is the distance from boundary node i to the nearest feature segment: g is gnear(`TX`[3i], `TX`[3i+1], `TX`[3i+2], 0, false); it returns infinity when g is below 0, otherwise sqrt(`R`[6]). `ofeat(fe bool)` snaps nodes to features: zf(`TX0`, the length of `TX`) and `TX0` becomes a copy of `TX` entry by entry; zu(`FMK`, `ONT`); `OFE` and `OCO` become 0. When fe is false it stops here. For a below `ON0` with `IB`[a] not 0, for each c of `BJ`[`BO`[a]] to `BJ`[`BO`[a] plus `BN`[a] minus 1]: when c is below a or `TF`[a] is not 0 or `TF`[c] is not 0, continue; ta is `TP`[a] and tc is `TP`[c]; when `TLB`[ta] equals `TLB`[tc] or `TLB`[ta] is below 0 or `TLB`[tc] is below 0, continue; da is ofd(ta) and dc is ofd(tc); when jmin(da, dc) is below `HBV`[a], `FMK`[ta when da is at most dc, otherwise tc] becomes 1. zf(`DS`, `ONT`); for i below `ONT` with `FMK`[i] not 0, `DS`[i] becomes ofd(i). For b below `ONQ`: the four nodes are t0 to t3 with ti equal to `TP`[`BQ`[5b plus i]]; repeat: w is −1 and cnt is 0; for each ti in order with `FMK`[ti] not 0: cnt grows by 1, and when w is −1 or `DS`[ti] is above `DS`[w], w becomes ti; when cnt is below 3, stop repeating; otherwise `FMK`[w] becomes 0. For i below `ONT` with `FMK`[i] not 0: ofd(i) is called and `TX`[3i], `TX`[3i+1], `TX`[3i+2] become `R`[0], `R`[1], `R`[2], and `OFE` grows by 1. For each v of `CO` in order: bi is −1 and bd2 is infinity; for i below `ONT` with `FMK`[i] not 0: ex is `TX`[3i] minus `V`[3v], ey is `TX`[3i+1] minus `V`[3v+1], ez is `TX`[3i+2] minus `V`[3v+2]; d is (ex·ex plus ey·ey) plus ez·ez; when d is below bd2, bd2 becomes d and bi becomes i. When bi is at least 0 and bd2 is below `OHC`·`OHC`: `FMK`[bi] becomes 2, `TX`[3bi], `TX`[3bi+1], `TX`[3bi+2] become `V`[3v], `V`[3v+1], `V`[3v+2] and `OCO` grows by 1.

The exported function `ms_tint()` interpolates the hanging boundary nodes: for each x of `TE` in order: n is `HN`[x]; sx, sy and sz start at 0.0; for i below `HC`[x]: v is `HM`[4x plus i] and w is `HW`[4x plus i]; sx becomes sx plus w·`TX`[3·`TP`[v]], sy becomes sy plus w·`TX`[3·`TP`[v]+1], sz becomes sz plus w·`TX`[3·`TP`[v]+2]; then `TX`[3·`TP`[n]] becomes sx, `TX`[3·`TP`[n]+1] becomes sy and `TX`[3·`TP`[n]+2] becomes sz.

`orn(n int) int` renumbers a node for output: when `US`[n] is below 0: `US`[n] becomes `ONN`, `ONN` grows by 1 and pf(`XO`, `XP`[3n]), pf(`XO`, `XP`[3n+1]), pf(`XO`, `XP`[3n+2]); it returns `US`[n]. `oasm()` assembles the elements: zi(`US`, `ON0`, −1); `XO`, `EL`, `CU` and `BF` are emptied; `ONN` becomes 0. For each e of `CEL` in order: pi(`EL`, orn(`E0`[8e+a])) for a from 0 to 7, then pf(`CU`, hzf(`LV`[`LF`[e]])). For n below `ON0` with `IB`[n] not 0, orn(n). `OT0` becomes `ONN`; pf(`XO`, 0.0) is called 3·`ONT` times; for n below `ON0` with `IB`[n] not 0, for c from 0 to 2, `XO`[3·(`OT0` plus `TP`[n]) plus c] becomes `TX`[3·`TP`[n] plus c]; `ONN` grows by `ONT`. For b below `ONQ`: pi(`EL`, orn(`BQ`[5b plus i])) for i from 0 to 3, then pi(`EL`, `OT0` plus `TP`[`BQ`[5b plus i]]) for i from 0 to 3, then pf(`CU`, 0.0) and pi(`BF`, 6·(`ONC` plus b) plus 1). `ONE` is the length of `EL` divided by 8.

`ohout()` lists the hanging-node constraints with 8 slots each: `HNO`, `HMO` and `HWO` are emptied and `OHD` becomes 0. For x below the length of `HN` with `HA`[x] equal to 1: n is `HN`[x] and m is `HC`[x]; when `US`[n] is at least 0 and m is at most 8 and `US`[`HM`[4x plus i]] is at least 0 for every i below m: pi(`HNO`, `US`[n]); for j from 0 to 7: pi(`HMO`, `US`[`HM`[4x plus j]] when j is below m, otherwise −1) and pf(`HWO`, `HW`[4x plus j] when j is below m, otherwise 0.0). Otherwise `OHD` grows by 1. Then for each x of `TE` in order: n is `HN`[x] and m is `HC`[x]; pi(`HNO`, `OT0` plus `TP`[n]); for j from 0 to 7: pi(`HMO`, `OT0` plus `TP`[`HM`[4x plus j]] when j is below m, otherwise −1) and pf(`HWO`, `HW`[4x plus j] when j is below m, otherwise 0.0).

`ohat(x real, y real, z real) real` is the size of the leaf holding a point: for l from `OL` down to 0: h is hzf(l); q is mget(`IK`, `IV`, okey(l, floor((x minus `OX`) divided by h) as an int, floor((y minus `OY`) divided by h) as an int, floor((z minus `OZ`) divided by h) as an int)); when q is at least 0 it returns h. It returns `OHC`. `tsort(n int)` stably sorts `TRO`[0] to `TRO`[n minus 1] ascending by the key `TRR`[x] of each entry x, as a bottom-up merge sort with zi(`TRW`, n, 0) as scratch: w starts at 1; while w is below n: lo starts at 0; while lo is below n: mid is the smaller of lo plus w and n, hi is the smaller of lo plus 2·w and n; i is lo, j is mid and k is lo; while k is below hi: when j is below hi and (i equals mid or the key of `TRO`[j] is below the key of `TRO`[i]), `TRW`[k] becomes `TRO`[j] and j grows by 1; otherwise `TRW`[k] becomes `TRO`[i] and i grows by 1; k grows by 1. Then for k from lo below hi, `TRO`[k] becomes `TRW`[k]; lo grows by 2·w. After each pass w doubles.

`ostats()` measures layers through the walls and writes `ORS`: `TRR`, `TRA` and `TRT` are emptied and at starts at 0.0. For t below `NT`: when `TH`[t] is NaN or infinite, or `AR`[t] is not above 0.0, continue; e is jmin(`TH`[t] divided by 2.0, `OHM` divided by 4.0); h is ohat(`C`[3t] minus `N`[3t]·e, `C`[3t+1] minus `N`[3t+1]·e, `C`[3t+2] minus `N`[3t+2]·e); pf(`TRR`, `TH`[t] divided by h), pf(`TRA`, `AR`[t]), pf(`TRT`, `TH`[t]); at becomes at plus `AR`[t]. nr is the length of `TRR`; zi(`TRO`, nr, 0) with `TRO`[x] equal to x; tsort(nr). thr is NaN when nr is 0, otherwise `TRR`[`TRO`[0]]; acc starts at 0.0; for each x of `TRO` in order: acc becomes acc plus `TRA`[x]; when acc is at least 0.02·at: thr becomes `TRR`[x] and stop. tmin starts at infinity and becomes jmin(tmin, `TRT`[x]) for each x of `TRO` in order. hmn starts at infinity, lmin at 99 and lmax at 0; for each q of `LF`: hmn becomes jmin(hmn, hzf(`LV`[q])), lmin becomes the smaller of lmin and `LV`[q], lmax the larger of lmax and `LV`[q]. zf(`ORS`, 24); `ORS`[0] to `ORS`[18] become, as reals: `OT0`, `ONC`, `ONT`, `ONL`, `ONB`, `ORF`, `ORY`, `OFE`, `OCO`, the length of `HNO`, `OHD`, thr, tmin, hmn, lmin, lmax, `ONE`, `ONN`, `OL`.

The exported function `ms_ofeat(nt i32, nc i32)` does zi(`PT`, nt, 0) and zi(`CO`, nc, 0) and reports `ms_view_i32(30, PT)` and `ms_view_i32(31, CO)`.

The exported function `ms_obudget(tgt i32, fixed i32)` sets `OBT` to tgt as an int, `OBG` to jmin(1500000, 4·tgt) as an int and `OFX` to fixed as an int.

The exported function `ms_oct(lx real, ly real, lz real, hx real, hy real, hz real, hc real, nlay real, stc real, hmin real, dl real, refine i32, nsm i32, fe i32) i32` builds the adaptive hex mesh of the current full surface, whose thicknesses are in `TH`. hc and stc are working copies of those arguments; `OFS` and `OFH` become 1.0. The steps from ex0 through the leaf loop run as a round, at most 4 rounds. ex0 is jmax(jmax(hx minus lx, hy minus ly), hz minus lz)·1.02 plus 1e-9; p is jmax(0.0, ceil(ms_log2(ex0 divided by hc) minus 1e-9)) as an int; pw starts at 1.0 and doubles p times; `OEX` is hc·pw; `OX` is (lx plus hx) divided by 2.0 minus `OEX` divided by 2.0, `OY` is (ly plus hy) divided by 2.0 minus `OEX` divided by 2.0, `OZ` is (lz plus hz) divided by 2.0 minus `OEX` divided by 2.0; `OHC` is hc. `OHM` starts at infinity; for t below `NT`: `HS`[t] becomes jmax(hmin, jmin(hc, (stc·`TH`[t]) divided by jmax(2.5, nlay plus 0.5))) and `OHM` becomes jmin(`OHM`, `HS`[t]). `OL` is jmin(12.0, jmax(1.0, ceil(ms_log2(`OEX` divided by `OHM` minus 1e-9)))) as an int and `OGR` is 1 shifted left by `OL`. `OQ` becomes 0; `LV`, `CI`, `CJ`, `CK`, `BDV`, `AL`, `SDV`, `OS` and `WK` are emptied; mnew(`IK`, `IV`, 1024), mnew(`SDK`, `SDI`, 1024); `OIU` and `OSU` become 0. pi(`OS`, 0) four times; while `OS` is not empty: k, j, i and l are popped from `OS` in that order (l was pushed first); c is ocls(l, i, j, k); when c equals 0.0, continue; when hzf(l) is above (c when c is above 0.0, otherwise hc)·1.0001 and l is below `OL`: for a from 0 to 7: pi(`OS`, l plus 1), pi(`OS`, 2·i plus (a and 1)), pi(`OS`, 2·j plus ((a shifted right by 1) and 1)), pi(`OS`, 2·k plus (a shifted right by 2)); otherwise oadd(l, i, j, k, c); and once `OBG` is above 0 and `OQ` is above `OBG`, `OS` is emptied so the loop ends. After a round, when `OBG` is above 0, `OQ` is above `OBG` and fewer than 4 rounds have run: f is sqrt(`OQ` as real divided by `OBT` as real); stc and `OFS` are multiplied by f; from the second such over-budget round on, when `OFX` is 0, hc and `OFH` are multiplied by exp(0.8·log(f)); and the next round runs. Otherwise the rounds end. When after the rounds `OBG` is above 0 and `OQ` is above `OBG`, ms_oct returns the negative of `OQ` as i32 at once, before anything below runs. Then pi(`WK`, q) for q from 0 below `OQ` and obal(). `ORF` becomes 0; rf starts at 0; loop: onodes(dl); when rf is at least refine, stop; when orefine() is false, stop; rf grows by 1. Then ocore(), ohang(), obq(), oadj(nsm), oproj(), ofeat(fe is not 0), ms_tint(), oasm(), ohout() and ostats() are called in that order. Then `ORS`[20] becomes `OFS` and `ORS`[21] becomes `OFH`. It reports `ms_view_i32(32, EL)`, `ms_view_f64(33, XO)`, `ms_view_f64(34, CU)`, `ms_view_i32(35, BF)`, `ms_view_f64(36, TX)`, `ms_view_f64(37, TX0)`, `ms_view_u8(38, FMK)`, `ms_view_i32(39, HNO)`, `ms_view_i32(40, HMO)`, `ms_view_f64(41, HWO)` and `ms_view_f64(42, ORS)`, and returns `ONE` as i32.
